image-plan:
  - file: The_judgement_of_the_dead_in_the_presence_of_Osiris.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: Hunefer's own judgment, painted on his funerary papyrus around 1275
      BCE. Anubis takes him into the judgment hall and weighs a portion of his
      soul, represented by his heart, while Ammit the Devourer awaits the
      result and Thoth records it. At the right the triumphant Hunefer, having
      passed the test, is presented by the falcon-headed Horus to Osiris,
      seated in his shrine with Isis, Nephthys and the four sons of Horus.
  - file: Coffin_of_Gua.jpg
    role: inline
    section: geography
    caption: "The Book of Two Ways, a Coffin Text: the two pathways in the right
      middle of this coffin are a map-like image of the Duat."
  - file: BD_Field_of_Hotep.jpg
    role: full
    section: geography
    caption: A'aru, 'The Field of Reeds', from the Papyrus of Ani — where the dead
      found virtuous were permitted to dwell, and where the harvests were
      believed never to be poor.
  - file: Ani_chap125.jpg
    role: inline
    section: housing-of-souls-and-afterlife
    caption: The 42 Judges of Ma'at, who sit as jury during the 'Weighing of the
      Heart'. The deceased had to address each of the forty-two Assessors of
      Maat by name.
  - file: Book_of_Gates_Barque_of_Ra_cropped.jpg
    role: full
    section: journey-of-the-sun
    caption: "Af or Afu, commonly known as Afu-Ra: the ram-headed form of Ra when
      travelling the Duat on the subterrestrial Nile — the 12 hours of night
      and the underworld — on the Mesektet barque, with Sia at the front, Heka
      behind, and the protective coiled serpent deity Mehen around them."
  - file: The_Weighing_of_the_Heart.svg
    role: inline
    section: differences-in-sources
    caption: "The Weighing of the Heart in the Duat, from a Book of the Dead
      written on papyrus: Anubis at the far right, the scales shown with the
      feather balance, and Ammit awaiting hearts that need to be devoured."
glossary:
  - term: Duat
    gloss: A concept in ancient Egyptian mythology involving death, most often
      seen as a realm where people go after they die.
    from: is a concept in ancient [[ Egyptian mythology ]] involving death. It is
      most often seen as a realm where people go after they die.
  - term: Shetit
    gloss: "The name most of the Duat's dark, gate-partitioned lands are
      identified by: a dangerous land translated as 'The Beyond'."
    from: These lands are described as being dark and were partitioned with a
      series of gates, and mostly identified as Shetit, a dangerous land
      translated as 'The Beyond'.[[ ^4 ]]
  - term: A'aru
    gloss: "'The Field of Reeds', where the dead found virtuous are permitted to
      dwell; described as a paradise where they could live their former life
      with their loved ones without pain or suffering."
    from: If the dead are found virtuous, they will be permitted to dwell in [[
      //A'aru// ]], also known as 'The Field of Reeds'.[[ ^12 ]] It is
      described as a paradise where the dead could live their former life with
      their loved ones without pain or suffering.
  - term: Ma'at
    gloss: Both the goddess of order and the conception of order, balance and
      truth itself; she is the feather that is weighed against the heart.
    from: "[[ Ma'at ]], who is both the goddess of order and the conception of
      order, balance and truth itself. She is the feather that is weighed
      against the heart in the 'Weighing of the Heart' ritual.[[ ^14 ]]"
  - term: Ammit
    gloss: The devourer of souls, who eats the hearts and souls of those who
      cannot pass the 'Weighing of the Heart' ritual.
    from: "[[ Ammit ]], of whom eats the hearts and souls of those who cannot pass
      the 'Weighing of the Heart' ritual. [...] was rejected and eaten by [[
      Ammit ]], the devourer of souls"
  - term: Amduat
    gloss: One of the funerary texts the Duat is known from; according to it the
      underworld consists of twelve regions signifying the twelve hours of the
      sun god's journey through it.
    from: What is known of the //Duat// derives principally from funerary texts
      such as the //[[ Book of Gates ]]//, the //[[ Book of Caverns ]]//, the
      //[[ Coffin Texts ]]//, the //[[ Amduat ]]//, and the //[[ Book of the
      Dead ]],// among many other sources.[[ ^2 ]][[ ^3 ]] [...] According to
      the //[[ Amduat ]]//, the underworld consists of twelve regions
      signifying the twelve hours of the sun god's journey through it
  - term: Af (Afu)
    gloss: The ram-headed form of Ra when travelling the Duat.
    from: //Af// or //Afu// (commonly known as //Afu-Ra//), the ram-headed form of
      Ra when traveling the //Duat// on the subterrestrial [[ Nile ]]
  - term: Book of the Dead
    gloss: "Also known as The Book of Coming Forth by Day: a collection of spells
      and prayers to protect the dead from the Duat's dangerous landscape, and
      one of the most studied texts in Egyptology."
    from: the //[[ Book of the Dead ]]// (also known as The Book of Coming Forth
      by Day) is considered to be an important and vital resource to
      understanding the //Duat// and is one of the most studied texts in
      Egyptology.[[ ^1 ]][[ ^11 ]] [...] The //[[ Book of the Dead ]]// and [[
      Coffin Texts ]] were collections of spells and prayers to protect the
      dead from the //Duat//'s dangerous landscape.
