anchors:
  hook: After confirming that they were sinless, the heart of the deceased was
    weighed by [[ Anubis ]] against the feather of [[ Maat ]], which
    represents truth and justice. Any heart that is heavier than the feather
    failed the test, and was rejected and eaten by [[ Ammit ]], the devourer
    of souls, as these people were denied existence after death in the Duat.
    [...] The //Duat// is not equivalent to the conceptions of [[ Hell ]] in
    the [[ Abrahamic religions ]], in which souls are condemned with fiery
    torment. The absolute punishment for the wicked, in ancient Egyptian
    thought, was the denial of an afterlife to the deceased, ceasing to exist
    in the intellectual form seen through the devouring of the heart by
    Ammit.[[ ^9 ]]
  standfirst: is a concept in ancient [[ Egyptian mythology ]] involving death.
    It is most often seen as a realm where people go after they die. [...] It
    is generally known best as a dark subterranean realm that not only houses
    the deceased, but a variety of [[ deities | deity ]].[[ ^4 ]] [...] The
    //Duat// is divided into sections by [[ twelve guarded gates |
    gate-deities-of-the-underworld ]] that represent each hour of the night
    and are closely associated with the journey of Ra and prominently feature
    [[ Osiris ]], god of the //Duat// and personification of rebirth.[[ ^8 ]]
