+++
The !!Duat!! or !!Tuat!! ([[ Ancient Egyptian | ancient-egypt ]]: [[ Hieroglyph | egyptian-hieroglyphs ]]: 𓇽 romanized: [[ dwꜣt | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dwꜣt#Egyptian ]]) is a concept in ancient [[ Egyptian mythology ]] involving death, most often seen as a realm where people go after they die, and generally known best as a dark subterranean realm that not only houses the deceased, but a variety of [[ deities | deity ]].[[ ^4 ]] Due to linguistic shifts within [[ Ancient Egypt ]], the //Duat// has also been called !!Te!! (Coptic: Ⲧⲏ, romanized: //Tē//) and !!Amenthes!! (Ancient Greek: Ἀμένθης, [[ romanized | romanization-of-ancient-greek ]]: //Aménthēs//).

What is known of it derives principally from funerary texts such as the //[[ Book of Gates ]]//, the //[[ Book of Caverns ]]//, the //[[ Coffin Texts ]]//, the //[[ Amduat ]]//, and the //[[ Book of the Dead ]],// among many other sources.[[ ^2 ]][[ ^3 ]] The deities those texts depict most commonly are [[ Osiris ]], [[ Anubis ]], [[ Thoth ]], [[ Horus ]], and [[ Maat ]], in various forms.[[ ^1 ]] All of these documents involve the //Duat//, but each of them fulfilled a different [[ purpose | ancient-egyptian-afterlife-beliefs ]] and depicts the realm in a variety of unique ways.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]]

== Overview

Two things happen in this realm more than anything else: the soul makes its journey and is judged after death, and the sun god [[ Ra ]] is reborn each night. The //Duat// is most often depicted as the setting for those rituals and [[ mythological | egyptian-mythology ]] events.[[ ^7 ]] It is divided into sections by [[ twelve guarded gates | gate-deities-of-the-underworld ]], one for each hour of the night. The gates are closely associated with the journey of Ra, and they prominently feature [[ Osiris ]], god of the //Duat// and personification of rebirth.[[ ^8 ]]

== Geography

The //Duat// met the material world at a particular place: burial chambers formed touching-points between the mundane world and the realm of the dead.[[ ^9 ]] The west bank of the [[ Nile ]] was associated with the dead, and [[ funeral barges | ancient-egyptian-funerary-practices ]] would mimic the sun god Ra's journey through the sky during the day.[[ ^10 ]]

The place itself is often described with features you could point to on a map — rivers, islands, fields, paths, and lakes — and then with features you could not: lakes of fire, walls of iron, and trees of turquoise.[[ ^2 ]] These lands are described as being dark and were partitioned with a series of gates, and mostly identified as Shetit, a dangerous land translated as 'The Beyond'.[[ ^4 ]] The other names run the same way. 'The Hidden Place' is described as a region where mortals and the divine are absent;[[ ^4 ]] 'Road of the Secret Things of Re-stau' is a road that Ra passes in his nightly journey.[[ ^7 ]]

For the deceased the important location was 'The Hall of Truth', often depicted alongside the '[[ Weighing of the Heart ]]' ritual in //Books of the Dead// and mentioned in the sixth hour of Ra's journey.[[ ^11 ]][[ ^7 ]] The dead found virtuous were permitted to dwell in [[ //A'aru// ]], also known as 'The Field of Reeds'.[[ ^12 ]] It is described as a paradise where the dead could live their former life with their loved ones without pain or suffering. Harvest of crops is often featured in depictions of //A'aru//, as it was believed that the harvests were never poor and the land in eternal spring.[[ ^12 ]]

== Deities

There are hundreds of different divinities of varying importance related to the //Duat// throughout time, with the most referenced being:

- [[ Osiris ]], who was believed to be the lord of the realm and personifies rebirth and the afterlife. In [[ his own mythology | osiris-myth ]], he himself is reborn after his brother [[ Seth | set-deity ]] slays him and Osiris's wife, [[ Isis ]] partially revives him. He is depicted as a man with green skin partially wrapped in bandages.[[ ^13 ]]
- [[ Anubis ]], son of Osiris and [[ Nephthys ]], who weighs the heart of the deceased. His domains also cover the [[ embalming ]] and mummification process as well as acting to guide souls to the //Duat// upon death. He is depicted with the black head of a canine, most often assumed to be a [[ jackal ]].[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Thoth ]], who records the verdict of the 'Weighing of the Heart'. He is most often depicted as an [[ Ibis ]] headed man with a stylus and tablet.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Horus ]], son of Osiris and Isis, who protects Ra during his journey through the //Duat// and is heir to Osiris.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Ma'at ]], who is both the goddess of order and the conception of order, balance and truth itself. She is the feather that is weighed against the heart in the 'Weighing of the Heart' ritual.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Ammit ]], who eats the hearts and souls of those who cannot pass the 'Weighing of the Heart' ritual. She is often depicted near the scales in //Books of the Dead// with the face of a crocodile, the mane and front half of a lion, and the hindquarters of a hippopotamus.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Ra ]], god of the sun who must journey through the //Duat// every night to be reborn every morning.[[ ^14 ]]
- [[ Apep ]], the personification of darkness and true chaos who attempts to devour Ra and bring about eternal chaos.[[ ^14 ]]

== Mythological significance



== Housing of souls and afterlife

To receive judgement the dead journeyed through the various parts of the Duat, and those who successfully passed the various challenges reached the //Judgment of the dead//. The first task in that ritual was to address each of the forty-two [[ Assessors of Maat ]] correctly, by name, while reciting the sins they did not commit during their lifetime.[[ ^15 ]] Only after confirming that they were sinless was the heart of the deceased weighed by [[ Anubis ]] against the feather of [[ Maat ]], which represents truth and justice. Any heart that is heavier than the feather failed the test, and was rejected and eaten by [[ Ammit ]], the devourer of souls, as these people were denied existence after death in the Duat. The souls that were lighter than the feather would pass this most important test, and would be allowed to travel to Aaru.

This is not damnation as later religions understood it. The //Duat// is not equivalent to the conceptions of [[ Hell ]] in the [[ Abrahamic religions ]], in which souls are condemned with fiery torment. The absolute punishment for the wicked, in ancient Egyptian thought, was the denial of an afterlife to the deceased, ceasing to exist in the intellectual form seen through the devouring of the heart by Ammit.[[ ^9 ]]

== Journey of the Sun

Each night Ra travelled through the //Duat//, bringing revivification to the dead as their main benefit. When in the underworld he was in his [[ ram | ovis-longipes-palaeoaegyptiacus ]]-headed form Af. Ra travelled under the world upon his [[ Atet ]] barge from west to east; on the course of the underground journey, he was transformed from his aged [[ Atum ]] form into his young [[ Khepri ]] form – the new dawning sun. The role of the dead king, worshiped as a god, was also central to the mythology surrounding the concept of Duat, often depicted as being identical with Ra.[[ ^16 ]]

The dead king travelled through the Duat, the Kingdom of Osiris, along with the sun god, using the special knowledge he was supposed to possess. That knowledge was recorded in the [[ Coffin Texts ]], which served as a guide to the hereafter not just for the king but for all deceased. According to the //[[ Amduat ]]//, the underworld consists of twelve regions signifying the twelve hours of the sun god's journey through it, battling [[ Apep ]] in order to bring order back to the earth in the morning. As his rays illuminated the Duat during the journey, they revived the dead who occupied the underworld and let them enjoy life after death during that hour of the night when they were in the presence of the sun god, after which they resumed their sleep, waiting for the god's return the following night.[[ ^5 ]]

== Differences in sources

While the //Duat// has a variety of depictions, the //[[ Book of the Dead ]]// (also known as The Book of Coming Forth by Day) is considered to be an important and vital resource to understanding the //Duat//, and is one of the most studied texts in Egyptology.[[ ^1 ]][[ ^11 ]] The conceptions of the //Duat// found in this text are therefore considered to be the most commonly referenced [[ canon | canon-fiction ]] when discussing [[ ancient Egyptian afterlife beliefs ]]. Both it and the [[ Coffin Texts ]] were collections of spells and prayers to protect the dead from the //Duat//'s dangerous landscape. None of the available sources that discuss the Duat set out to lay out a geography or a coherent setting; what they describe is a succession of rites of passage which the dead would have to pass to reach eternal life.[[ ^5 ]]

== In popular culture

- The Duat appears as a major location in [[ Rick Riordan ]]'s //[[ The Kane Chronicles ]]// series. It is revealed in //[[ The Crown of Ptolemy ]]// to have connections to the Mist from Riordan's //[[ Camp Half-Blood Chronicles ]]// series.
- In //[[ Assassin's Creed Origins ]]//, the Duat is an important aspect of the game with the main character, [[ Bayek ]], entering it multiple times.
- The Duat makes an appearance in the episode "[[ Asylum | asylum-moon-knight ]]" of the [[ Disney+ ]] series //[[ Moon Knight | moon-knight-miniseries ]]// (2022) as the place where [[ Marc Spector | marc-spector-marvel-cinematic-universe ]] and his alter [[ Steven Grant | steven-grant-marvel-cinematic-universe ]] are being guided by the Egyptian goddess [[ Taweret | taweret-marvel-cinematic-universe ]] in the afterlife after being killed by [[ Arthur Harrow ]].[[ ^17 ]]
- In seminal Beat Generation author William S. Burroughs' work //[[ The Western Lands ]]//, the time travelling, anti hero gunslinger, Kim Carsons explores the Duat and confronts its inhabitants.[[ ^18 ]]

